[{:db/id 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-276f25e8-0fe6-5cab-8eeb-f55147c6c339"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bacbcea3-6ad6-5840-9bf7-1e8371e0974d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-276f25e8-0fe6-5cab-8eeb-f55147c6c339" :question/id #uuid "276f25e8-0fe6-5cab-8eeb-f55147c6c339" :question/math-academy-id "q-114039" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1+6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $6{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{6}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{6}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+6{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(\\sqrt{6}x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a430fc00-ecdb-5355-9c79-98aa358084c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a430fc00-ecdb-5355-9c79-98aa358084c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "a430fc00-ecdb-5355-9c79-98aa358084c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b31a011-34d6-54fb-97d3-f5fd61f6b537" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68c27e18-cd86-5136-97ff-930ab97daa47" "answer-bbcb8ff0-bcc8-5c73-8990-398286767616" "answer-3b31a011-34d6-54fb-97d3-f5fd61f6b537" "answer-ff2fa96a-ca7c-53d2-b91f-002c52d75021" "answer-74bba607-5459-5096-9775-251242509188"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68c27e18-cd86-5136-97ff-930ab97daa47" :answer/id #uuid "68c27e18-cd86-5136-97ff-930ab97daa47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbcb8ff0-bcc8-5c73-8990-398286767616" :answer/id #uuid "bbcb8ff0-bcc8-5c73-8990-398286767616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b31a011-34d6-54fb-97d3-f5fd61f6b537" :answer/id #uuid "3b31a011-34d6-54fb-97d3-f5fd61f6b537" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff2fa96a-ca7c-53d2-b91f-002c52d75021" :answer/id #uuid "ff2fa96a-ca7c-53d2-b91f-002c52d75021" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74bba607-5459-5096-9775-251242509188" :answer/id #uuid "74bba607-5459-5096-9775-251242509188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6}x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bacbcea3-6ad6-5840-9bf7-1e8371e0974d" :question/id #uuid "bacbcea3-6ad6-5840-9bf7-1e8371e0974d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114075" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/9)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/9}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae824470-993a-5443-bdc9-a07993674d20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae824470-993a-5443-bdc9-a07993674d20" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae824470-993a-5443-bdc9-a07993674d20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea5fd737-b612-553b-8e19-91de7f399fb5" "answer-abc0b7d1-6dfb-5790-a262-2aa331de3a0a" "answer-15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707" "answer-83a4d996-c213-5d67-946a-4cd6fe97edb9" "answer-bb2aeebc-c3cc-5f32-bec9-7d9027991243"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea5fd737-b612-553b-8e19-91de7f399fb5" :answer/id #uuid "ea5fd737-b612-553b-8e19-91de7f399fb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abc0b7d1-6dfb-5790-a262-2aa331de3a0a" :answer/id #uuid "abc0b7d1-6dfb-5790-a262-2aa331de3a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707" :answer/id #uuid "15391250-e023-5a04-9057-9658fe4de707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83a4d996-c213-5d67-946a-4cd6fe97edb9" :answer/id #uuid "83a4d996-c213-5d67-946a-4cd6fe97edb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb2aeebc-c3cc-5f32-bec9-7d9027991243" :answer/id #uuid "bb2aeebc-c3cc-5f32-bec9-7d9027991243" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186377539 :answer-field/correct "answer-35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324" :answer/id #uuid "35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065433 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065381 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064411 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065076 :step/math-academy-id 641164 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065077 :step/math-academy-id 641165 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065078 :step/math-academy-id 641168 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065124 :step/math-academy-id 641171 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065079 :step/math-academy-id 641174 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065182 :step/math-academy-id 641177 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
